Here is a Java application that generates and displays all prime numbers in the range of 1 to n, where n is a specified integer.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 20;
        List<Integer> primes = getPrimes(n);
        System.out.println("Prime numbers in the range of 1 to " + n + " are:");
        for (int i : primes) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }

    public static List<Integer> getPrimes(int n) {
        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
        for(int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            if(isPrime(i)) {
                primes.add(i);
            }
        }
        return primes;
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return false;
        for(int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
            if(n % i == 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

This program works by first defining a `main` method that calls a `getPrimes` method with an integer `n` as a parameter. This method generates a list of prime numbers in the range of 1 to `n` using the `isPrime` method. Then, it prints all prime numbers in the list.

The `isPrime` method checks if a number is prime by testing whether it is divisible by any number other than 1 and itself. It does this by iterating from 2 to the square root of the number, and returning false if it finds a divisor. If no divisors are found, it returns true.

Finally, the `main` method prints all prime numbers in the list using a for-each loop.

Please note that this program assumes that the input is a positive integer. If the input is not a positive integer, it will result in an incorrect output.

One of the most effective ways to segregate these two types of tests in Go is to utilize tags in the `go test` command. Here's how you can do it:

1. Create a file named `foo_test.go` which contains your hermetic, non-API-key-dependent tests.
2. Create a file named `api_key_test.go` which contains your API-key-dependent tests.
3. Use the `-tags=api_key` flag in the `go test` command to run the tests in `api_key_test.go` when you need to.

Here's an example of how you can do it:

```
go test -tags=api_key
```

This command will only run tests in `api_key_test.go`, which are the API-key-dependent tests. Note that you don't need to pass any tags to run the hermetic tests in `foo_test.go`, so if you don't pass `-tags=api_key`, it will run both sets of tests.

In `api_key_test.go`:

```go
// Tests that need an API key can be tagged here.
func TestAPIKeyDependentTest(t *testing.T) {
    // Your API-key-dependent test code here
}
```

In `foo_test.go`:

```go
// Tests that don't need an API key can be tagged here.
func TestHermeticTest(t *testing.T) {
    // Your hermetic test code here
}
```

Remember to replace `TestAPIKeyDependentTest` and `TestHermeticTest` with the actual names of your tests.

By segregating your tests in this way, you can run them selectively whenever you need, keeping your hermetic tests and API-key-dependent tests separate, which is especially useful when you want to run the hermetic tests in CI.

You can use java.sql to fetch the result from the MySQL database. Here is an example:

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.sql.Statement;

public class FetchCountFromDB {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Connection conn = null;
        Statement stmt = null;
        ResultSet rs = null;
        int count = 0;
        try {
            conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/database_name", "username", "password");
            stmt = conn.createStatement();
            rs = stmt.executeQuery("select count(*) from employeedetails");
            if (rs.next()) {
                count = rs.getInt(1);
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            try {
                if (rs != null) rs.close();
                if (stmt != null) stmt.close();
                if (conn != null) conn.close();
            } catch (SQLException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
        System.out.println(count);
    }
}
```

Please replace "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/database_name", "username", "password" with your database credentials.

Please make sure the MySQL JDBC driver is included in your project.

